id: recipe.resilient-dependent-service
kind: recipe
title: Service calling an unreliable dependency
aliases: resilience against dependency, flaky upstream, degraded mode
question: how would you design a service that depends on an unreliable third party?
tags: architecture, resilience, reliability
properties:
  - bounded waiting on the dependency
  - fail fast when it is clearly down
  - a useful degraded answer instead of an error
  - no retry amplification
invariant: Treat the dependency as a failure source by default: bound every call, retry only idempotent operations with jitter, break the circuit, and have a degraded path that does not need it.
answer_30s: Timeout every call, retry idempotently with jitter under a strict budget, open a breaker when the failure rate is high, and serve a cached or partial answer so the user still gets something useful.
flow: |
  request -> caller
     |
  budget: deadline (200ms) shared with the dependency
     |
  breaker closed? --no--> degraded response (cache / partial / queued)
     | yes
  attempt with timeout -> retry (jitter, cap 3)
     |
  failure rate over threshold -> breaker opens -> fail fast
     |
  metrics: timeout rate, breaker state, degraded rate
compose:
  - design.timeouts => the deadline that bounds all waiting
  - design.retry-backoff => bounded, jittered retries on idempotent calls only
  - design.circuit-breaker => stop paying the timeout cost when it is clearly down
  - design.idempotency => safe retries and safe replay after recovery
  - design.load-shedding => protect yourself when the dependency is slow, not just down
  - design.observability => timeout rate and breaker state as first-class signals
  - design.cache-aside => a stale cached answer is often the right degraded response
  - rust.retry => the implementation shape of classified retries
  - rust.timeout => the per-attempt bound
  - rust.tower => the same policy for every outbound call
failure_modes:
  - retries without idempotency, duplicating the effect on a timeout
  - no timeout, so a hanging dependency consumes the connection pool
  - a degraded path that also calls the broken dependency
  - layers of retries multiplying load onto a struggling dependency
  - breaker that never closes because the threshold is absolute rather than rate-based
follow_ups:
  - the dependency is down for an hour: how does your error budget and user experience evolve?
related:
  - design.circuit-breaker
  - design.retry-backoff
  - design.timeouts
sources:
  - https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/circuit-breaker
  - https://sre.google/sre-book/addressing-cascading-failures/
